00:15:31.480 --> 00:15:32.420
and that people are still

586
00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:35.180
as enthusiastic about it as they are,

587
00:15:35.180 --> 00:15:36.440
thanks to streaming

588
00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:37.860
and the fact that we can continue

589
00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:38.620
to have generations

590
00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:40.500
that are just discovering it now.

591
00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:42.860
It's extraordinary to have fans

592
00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:44.860
that are in their 60s and 70s

593
00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:46.200
and fans that are 10.

594
00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:48.120
You know, right?

595
00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:49.380
It's really crazy.

596
00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:50.200
Yeah.

597
00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:51.800
And I had been, you know,

598
00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:52.500
in this business

599
00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:53.480
since I was six years old.

600
00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:55.280
And I think that when I did the office,

601
00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:56.240
I think I literally,

602
00:15:56.440 --> 00:15:57.740
I think it was my 13th pilot.

603
00:15:58.060 --> 00:15:58.420
Wow.

604
00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:00.500
And so I was really used to things

605
00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:01.760
just, you know, being on the air

606
00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:02.600
as long as they were on the air.

607
00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:03.460
And then that was it,

608
00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:04.500
just going off the air

609
00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:06.360
or just getting put up on a shelf,

610
00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:07.360
no one ever seeing them

611
00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:08.420
or some people seeing them

612
00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:09.780
or maybe a few people seeing them

613
00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:11.600
or even when it was kind of like successful,

614
00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:13.040
but then it was over

615
00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:14.080
and that's it, you know?

616
00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:16.560
So this was all very shocking to me.

617
00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:20.000
And just where I was in my reality timeline

618
00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:22.680
of, you know, my age and stuff

619
00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:23.720
and in my life,

620
00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:27.800
this was just a very unusual experience for me.

621
00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:28.560
I don't know if I've,

622
00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:29.880
if I told you guys about that

623
00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:31.260
when that I didn't even believe

624
00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:31.980
we were hit.

625
00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:33.620
Did I tell you that?

626
00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:35.400
I was like,

627
00:16:35.540 --> 00:16:36.720
I didn't know we were a hit

628
00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:38.960
until we won the SAG award,

629
00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:41.640
the first SAG award.

630
00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:43.720
And I don't know if you remember

631
00:16:43.720 --> 00:16:46.100
that trophy is made out of bronze

632
00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:47.420
and then the base of it

633
00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:49.600
is just a solid brick of marble.

634
00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:50.660
Marble, heavy, heavy.

635
00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:51.960
And when you are a winner, right,

636
00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:53.040
you walk around all night,

637
00:16:53.180 --> 00:16:54.980
you don't carry it like at your knee.

638
00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:56.560
You don't carry it above your head.

639
00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:58.920
You carry it with your elbow bent up here

640
00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:00.840
because everyone wants to see it.

641
00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:01.840
So you're kind of holding it

642
00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:03.420
like right at your shoulder, right?

643
00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:05.460
I woke up the next morning

644
00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:08.220
and I could not lift my arm.

645
00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:10.880
My bicep was just absolutely,

646
00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:12.460
completely dead.

647
00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:15.060
And I literally needed physical proof.

648
00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:15.600
I was like,

649
00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:17.660
oh, I guess that means we're hit.

650
00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.380
I mean, it was just that simple for me.

651
00:17:20.820 --> 00:17:23.880
I don't know whether it's the dancer in me or what,

652
00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:25.400
but I needed physical proof.

653
00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:26.860
A muscle memory of like,

654
00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:29.360
oh, this pain means it happened.

655
00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:32.480
Yes, it means I was carrying an award around all night

656
00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:34.260
and we are a hit.

657
00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:36.160
It was just surreal to me.

658
00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:37.480
Really surreal.

659
00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:38.340
Anyway, sorry.

660
00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:39.720
You didn't even ask me one question

661
00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:40.600
and I went on a rant.

662
00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:41.640
This is what it is.

663
00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:42.480
This is what we love.

664
00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:44.300
So many people wrote in.

665
00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:45.420
We put out a prompt.

666
00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:46.860
Hey, Melora's going to be on the show.

667
00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:48.140
Do you have any questions for Jan?

668
00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.440
Melora, we got so many amazing questions.

669
00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:50.860
Oh, good.

670
00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:52.820
So we're just going to throw a few at you today.

671
00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:53.420
Okay, good.

672
00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:53.880
Yeah, sure.

673
00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:57.020
Let's kick things off with Peyton W.

674
00:17:57.320 --> 00:17:58.220
from Hiram, Georgia,

675
00:17:58.440 --> 00:17:58.940
who said,

676
00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:00.860
when first starting the role,

677
00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:03.980
did Melora create any kind of character background

678
00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:06.240
apart from what was scripted in the show?

679
00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:08.500
And then Lisa R.

680
00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:10.800
from Plymouth Lake, California said,

681
00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:13.040
what is Jan's history?

682
00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:15.900
We first learn about her past at Chili's,

683
00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:18.800
but who is Mr. Gould and what is that backstory?

684
00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:22.180
Did you create any kind of backstory for Jan?

685
00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:24.080
I don't think so.

686
00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.240
I don't remember that I did that.

687
00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:29.700
You know, Jan was so visceral to me.

688
00:18:29.960 --> 00:18:33.800
You know, Steve and I had such an interesting chemistry

689
00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:37.420
that when we were playing the characters,

690
00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:41.120
there was such a distinct push-pull,

691
00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:43.480
like attraction repulsion,

692
00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:46.420
that was so visceral to me

693
00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:48.640
that I really just hooked into that.

694
00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:51.800
And I think that's really how Greg Daniels,

695
00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:53.080
the writers, you know,

696
00:18:53.660 --> 00:18:55.420
wrote, built the character,

697
00:18:55.780 --> 00:19:00.380
was really from that sort of tactile kind of experience

698
00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:02.940
that I think the audience can feel

699
00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:04.440
and I think Steve and I could feel.

700
00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:08.040
And I remember saying it out loud at lunch during the pilot.

701
00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:10.020
I remember saying, you know,

702
00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:11.840
God, if we get picked up,

703
00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:14.800
Michael and Jan have to hook up somehow, some way.

704
00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:17.500
And I remember Steve laughing and Greg laughing so hard

705
00:19:17.500 --> 00:19:18.160
and me laughing.

706
00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:18.840
And we were all just like,

707
00:19:18.940 --> 00:19:21.540
oh my God, that would be so wrong and so right.

708
00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:26.420
Like we all just got it so early

709
00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:28.960
that I don't even think there was time

710
00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:31.120
to even think that way about this character,

711
00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:31.560
this role.

712
00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:33.180
I was hired as a guest star,

713
00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:35.760
possible recurring character in the pilot.

714
00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:39.180
I was not even necessarily going to be a regular on this show.

715
00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:42.520
I had done so many guest star,

716
00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:44.460
possible recurring characters in my career.

717
00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:46.520
So as far as like,

718
00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:47.760
is this going to be, you know,

719
00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:49.040
some memorable thing?

720
00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:53.620
I just was going in with this visceral feeling I had for her

721
00:19:53.620 --> 00:19:55.660
and it seemed to be working.

722
00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:56.580
Everybody liked it.

723
00:19:56.640 --> 00:19:57.220
They were laughing.

724
00:19:57.540 --> 00:19:58.600
They were enjoying it.

725
00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:00.200
Steve and I had great chemistry

726
00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:01.540
when we were working together

727
00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:02.180
and it was like,

728
00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:03.180
let's just go with this.

729
00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:05.620
Well, I, you know,

730
00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:08.020
a lot of people did write in about the chemistry.

731
00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:08.600
Okay.

732
00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:10.000
And Steve F.

733
00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:11.420
from Bryan, Texas said,

734
00:20:11.620 --> 00:20:15.080
do you remember a first or early moment on set

735
00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:17.780
that you knew you and Steve had great chemistry?

736
00:20:17.880 --> 00:20:19.400
It sounds like right from the pilot,

737
00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:21.320
but Steve wants you to know

738
00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:24.200
that your relationship on camera was truly remarkable.

739
00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:25.760
Oh, that's so nice.

740
00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:26.220
Yeah.

741
00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:26.740
Yeah.

742
00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:27.520
I mean, I felt it.

743
00:20:27.620 --> 00:20:29.660
I think Steve and I both felt it really early.

744
00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:32.440
I have come from a background in improv,

745
00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:35.140
but different kind of improv than what Steve had come from.

746
00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:37.180
You know, my mother was an acting teacher

747
00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:39.440
and she was an actress herself as a younger woman.

748
00:20:39.640 --> 00:20:42.100
And then she kind of put together her own technique

749
00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:43.540
and she taught young people.

750
00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:44.720
She taught kids.

751
00:20:44.900 --> 00:20:46.180
Her name was Diane Hill Harden.

752
00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:48.200
She taught Leonardo DiCaprio when he was a kid

753
00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:50.260
and River Phoenix and Joaquin Phoenix

754
00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:52.580
and Maggie Gyllenhaal and Jake Gyllenhaal

755
00:20:52.580 --> 00:20:55.640
and Jessica Biel and, you know, on and on.

756
00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:59.520
But the point being that I think that the kind of improv

757
00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:02.020
I came from was really playing the truth.

758
00:21:02.240 --> 00:21:04.020
So not going for the laughs,

759
00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:06.880
but finding the comedy inside the truth.

760
00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:09.320
So that was why as a kid, actually,

761
00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:11.660
I think I was thought of mostly as a dramatic actress.

762
00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:14.560
And then as I got older and then, you know,

763
00:21:14.980 --> 00:21:16.840
comedy happened for me, people were like,

764
00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:18.000
oh, wow, she's really funny.

765
00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:20.760
But I think that's where the chemistry comes from, really.

766
00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:24.940
It literally is just that I say, Steve can throw a ball.

767
00:21:25.140 --> 00:21:26.140
It could be a curve ball.

768
00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:29.800
And I would get so excited by that and catch it.

769
00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:31.820
And then he could throw a straight ball the next time

770
00:21:31.820 --> 00:21:33.260
or an underhanded ball the next time

771
00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:35.000
or an overhanded ball the next time.

772
00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:38.440
And I was just always like ready to catch those balls.

773
00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:40.120
And he was exactly the same way.

774
00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:42.080
So I think we had a lot of fun.

775
00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:43.440
I certainly did.

776
00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:45.860
I can't speak for Steve, but it felt like we both did.

777
00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:47.520
But I certainly had a lot of fun

778
00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:50.060
tossing those balls in different ways,

779
00:21:50.060 --> 00:21:52.000
even subtly all the time.

780
00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:54.300
And I think that does come from a comfort

781
00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:57.620
with the unknown, a comfort with improv.

782
00:21:57.940 --> 00:21:59.480
Well, to continue that metaphor,

783
00:21:59.900 --> 00:22:02.360
I like this metaphor of the idea of like pitchers

784
00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:03.840
and catchers in improv.

785
00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:06.580
And I sort of saw myself on the show

786
00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:09.720
as more of a catcher of other people's improv.

787
00:22:09.920 --> 00:22:12.200
But I think you have,

788
00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:15.220
what makes Jan so fun is that you're both.

789
00:22:15.580 --> 00:22:18.080
I think Jan was a pitcher and a catcher

790
00:22:18.080 --> 00:22:19.360
because I would see it.

791
00:22:19.360 --> 00:22:20.280
Oh, interesting.

792
00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:21.180
On set as well.

793
00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:23.300
Like you just toss it right back to him,

794
00:22:23.480 --> 00:22:25.300
you know, but you catch it just right too.

795
00:22:25.700 --> 00:22:26.620
So thanks.

796
00:22:26.900 --> 00:22:27.000
Yeah.

797
00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:27.600
What fun.

798
00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:29.100
Yeah, it is fun.

799
00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:30.500
And that's a really great observation

800
00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:31.240
about your character.

801
00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:32.920
I feel like you're totally right about that.

802
00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:34.880
And that was what made her so special too.

803
00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:38.260
Because she just was so always available for that

804
00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:40.540
from anybody, you know?

805
00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:40.940
Yeah.

806
00:22:40.960 --> 00:22:41.680
So great.

807
00:22:41.900 --> 00:22:42.520
So great.

808
00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:42.860
Yeah.

809
00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:44.820
I think the thing that really spoke to me

810
00:22:44.820 --> 00:22:46.320
and everything you said there

811
00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:47.920
was just playing the truth.

812
00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:50.720
Because that's the highest brow of comedy.

813
00:22:51.020 --> 00:22:51.220
Truly.

814
00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:52.600
It's not going for the joke.

815
00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:55.500
That's the cheapest way to go about getting a laugh

816
00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:56.480
is to go for the joke.

817
00:22:56.640 --> 00:22:57.640
But if you play the truth,

818
00:22:58.260 --> 00:23:00.700
it's so pure and real.

819
00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:03.260
And you did that so many times as Jan.

820
00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:04.480
I was watching you,

821
00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:05.860
oh my gosh, last night.

822
00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:09.580
There was a moment where Jan just finally decides

823
00:23:09.580 --> 00:23:11.880
she's going to get back together with Michael.

824
00:23:12.060 --> 00:23:12.280
Yeah.

825
00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:16.140
Even though he has sent the email to everyone,

826
00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:18.700
you know, Jan Tan everywhere, Jan everywhere.

827
00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:20.580
Like it's gone so far.

828
00:23:20.860 --> 00:23:22.940
And as she's like,

829
00:23:23.060 --> 00:23:25.660
look, I don't know why my therapist told me

830
00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:26.660
just lean into this,

831
00:23:26.820 --> 00:23:28.080
but clearly I'm attracted to you.

832
00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:28.660
I don't know why.

833
00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:31.460
And Michael is trying to say back to you

834
00:23:31.460 --> 00:23:32.200
that he agrees,

835
00:23:32.380 --> 00:23:33.740
but he's so excited.

836
00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:35.280
And it comes out like,

837
00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:40.120
and I also henceforth do the thereby

838
00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:42.180
affirm this moment.

839
00:23:42.280 --> 00:23:43.660
Like whatever, I'm paraphrasing it.

840
00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:46.200
But he says so many ridiculous things in a row

841
00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:47.040
saying yes.

842
00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:49.400
And then you have this moment

843
00:23:49.400 --> 00:23:50.300
where you just go,

844
00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:51.140
huh?

845
00:23:53.660 --> 00:23:55.900
I mean, those little moments

846
00:23:55.900 --> 00:23:57.300
of you reacting to him

847
00:23:57.300 --> 00:23:59.580
are so truthful as Jan.

848
00:23:59.820 --> 00:24:01.800
Some of my favorites are your nonverbals.

849
00:24:01.820 --> 00:24:02.640
Yes, yes.

850
00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:04.800
There's an elevator nonverbal

851
00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:06.300
that's literally like,

852
00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:08.600
I'll do it with you when I see it.

853
00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:10.400
It's the one where you're like,

854
00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:12.800
Michael, you're a good looking guy,

855
00:24:12.980 --> 00:24:13.900
but I just this,

856
00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:14.800
and you're annoying,

857
00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:16.180
and I can't stand you.

858
00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:17.780
And you say all these insults,

